---
title: Dal Tadka
description: North Indian restaurant-style lentils simmered with an onion and tomato masala, finished with a tempering of cumin, garlic and chillies bloomed in hot ghee.
author: Swasthi Shreekanth
source: https://www.indianhealthyrecipes.com/dal-tadka/
course: main
cuisine: indian
diet: vegetarian
difficulty: easy
servings: 4
time: 40m
prep time: 10m
cook time: 30m
tags: [lentils, curry, pressure-cooker]
---

= Dal =

Add @toor dal{150%g}, @moong dal{50%g} and @chana dal{2%tbsp} to a #pressure cooker{}. Rinse at least four times, rubbing the lentils between your fingers, until the water runs clear. Drain completely.

Add @water{720%ml}, close the cooker and cook on medium heat for 3 to 4 whistles. Let the pressure drop on its own before opening.

> No pressure cooker: soak the rinsed dal in the cooking water, hot, for at least 30 minutes. Bring to a boil, then simmer uncovered for 20 to 30 minutes until soft and mushy, skimming off any scum and adding hot water if it dries out. Instant Pot: 10 minutes on high pressure.

> 200 g toor dal on its own also works. You will need less water to loosen it later.

The dal should be soft and mushy, with the chana dal still holding a little texture. Mash lightly so the chana dal does not dissolve completely. If it is too thick, loosen it with up to @water{180%ml}, boiling. Older lentils need more.

= Masala =

While the dal cooks, heat @oil{2%tbsp} in a #medium pan{}. Add @cumin seeds{1/2%tsp} and fry until they splutter.

Add @garlic cloves{4} and @ginger{2.5%cm}, both finely chopped, and fry on low heat until fragrant. Do not let them brown.

Add @onion{1}, finely chopped, and @green chilli{1}, slit or chopped (optional; leave it out if serving kids). Fry, stirring now and then, until the onion is translucent and just starting to turn golden.

Reduce the heat. Stir in @turmeric{1/4%tsp}, @red chilli powder{1/2%tsp} and @garam masala{1/2%tsp}.

Add @tomatoes{2}, deseeded and finely chopped, with @salt{1%tsp}. Sauté until the tomatoes are soft and the mixture starts to smell good.

Tip the masala into the cooked dal and simmer ~{6%minutes}, up to 7, so the flavours blend. Adjust to the consistency you want, keeping in mind the dal thickens as it cools. Taste and add more @salt{to taste}.

Crush @kasuri methi{1%tbsp} between your palms over the boiling dal and stir well. Add @coriander leaves{2%tbsp}, finely chopped, and turn off the heat.

= Smoke (optional) =

Place a small #steel cup{} in the centre of the dal. Hold a piece of @lump charcoal{1} with #tongs over an open flame for about 2 minutes, until part of it glows red. Set it in the cup and pour @ghee{1/2%tsp} over it.

Cover the pot immediately and let the dal smoke ~{3%minutes}, up to 4. Remove the cup.

> This dhungar step gives the smoky restaurant flavour. The dal is still full of flavour without it.

= Tadka =

Transfer the dal to a serving bowl. Melt @ghee{2%tbsp} in a #small pan{} over medium heat. Add @cumin seeds{3/4%tsp} and, if using, @mustard seeds{1/4%tsp}. Fry on low heat until they crackle and smell aromatic.

Stir in @dried red chillies{2}, then @garlic cloves{4}, finely chopped, and, if using, @curry leaves{1%sprig}, patted dry. Fry until the garlic is light and aromatic. Do not brown it, or it turns bitter.

Stir in @asafoetida{1/8%tsp} and @red chilli powder{1/3%tsp}, take the pan off the heat straight away and pour the hot tadka over the dal.

Garnish with more @coriander leaves{garnish} and squeeze over @lemon{1} as you serve. Serve with @basmati rice{300%g}, or with roti, naan or paratha.

> Instead of lemon, you can stir amchur (dried mango powder) in with the kasuri methi, about a teaspoon, for a fruity tang.

> For less heat, use Kashmiri chilli powder.
